From 50030794b955e4a23d08e77b04cbb7a9191cb49d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomoatsu Shimada <shimada@walbrix.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 09:54:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] hwaccel-nvidia: Skip CUDA/NVENC when no NVIDIA device is
 present

The generic (hardware-agnostic) genpack GNOME artifact bundles the NVIDIA
userspace driver (libcuda) so images work on machines that do have an
NVIDIA GPU. On machines *without* one, libcuda is still present and
dlopen-able, so grd_hwaccel_nvidia_new() loads it and calls cuInit().

cuInit() fork()s internally (to run nvidia-modprobe / create the
/dev/nvidia* nodes when missing). grd calls it from RDP server startup,
by which point the EGL thread and the Mesa render worker threads
(radeonsi/anv: cs0, disk$0, sh0, gdrv0, gl0, ...) are already running.
Forking from that multi-threaded state leaves the child holding locks
owned by threads that do not exist in the child, so the child deadlocks
(futex) in its own exit path and the parent blocks forever in wait4().
The RDP server never finishes starting; in the login-screen handover
path StartHandover() is thus never called, the client is never
redirected, and after the 30 s handover timeout the connection is
dropped -- i.e. remote login is completely broken (black screen then
disconnect).

Machines that actually have an NVIDIA GPU are unaffected: the kernel
module is loaded and /dev/nvidiactl exists, so cuInit() does not need to
fork and CUDA/NVENC (still the only H.264 encode path for NVIDIA) keeps
working.

Guard the NVIDIA hwaccel bring-up on the presence of /dev/nvidiactl: if
there is no NVIDIA kernel driver there is no usable NVIDIA GPU, so skip
CUDA/NVENC and let grd_rdp_server_new() fall back to Vulkan/VA-API.
---
 src/grd-hwaccel-nvidia.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/grd-hwaccel-nvidia.c b/src/grd-hwaccel-nvidia.c
index b4640e0..5dfe404 100644
--- a/src/grd-hwaccel-nvidia.c
+++ b/src/grd-hwaccel-nvidia.c
@@ -829,6 +829,20 @@ grd_hwaccel_nvidia_new (GrdEglThread *egl_thread)
   CUresult cu_result;
   unsigned int i;
 
+  /* On hardware without an NVIDIA GPU the bundled libcuda is still loadable,
+   * but cuInit() fork()s internally (to run nvidia-modprobe) and, when called
+   * from grd's multi-threaded state (EGL/Mesa render threads already running),
+   * the forked child deadlocks -- hanging RDP server startup and, in the login
+   * handover path, blocking remote login entirely.  If no NVIDIA kernel driver
+   * is present (no /dev/nvidiactl) there is no usable NVIDIA GPU, so skip
+   * CUDA/NVENC and let the caller fall back to Vulkan/VA-API. */
+  if (!g_file_test ("/dev/nvidiactl", G_FILE_TEST_EXISTS))
+    {
+      g_debug ("[HWAccel.CUDA] No NVIDIA kernel driver present "
+               "(/dev/nvidiactl missing); skipping CUDA/NVENC initialization");
+      return NULL;
+    }
+
   hwaccel_nvidia = g_object_new (GRD_TYPE_HWACCEL_NVIDIA, NULL);
   hwaccel_nvidia->egl_thread = egl_thread;
 
-- 
